/-! # Flat ambient neighbourhood of an H₁-regular flat quotient

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 063V (lemma-flat-relative-H1-regular), conclusion (1).
(blueprint: lem-sfb-h1-flat-quotient-ambient-flat-neighbourhood)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace RingTheory

/-- A finitely presented algebra with an H₁-regular sequence and flat quotient is flat
on a principal neighbourhood of every prime containing the sequence ideal.

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 063V, lemma-flat-relative-H1-regular, conclusion (1).
(blueprint: lem-sfb-h1-flat-quotient-ambient-flat-neighbourhood)

Proof:
1. Choose a finite polynomial presentation of B and polynomial lifts of f. By
   lemma-nagata-ring-finite-type-subalgebras, descend their coefficients to a finite type
   ℤ-subalgebra A₀ of A. Define B₀ and its marked quotient C₀ by these presentations.
   lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-scalar-extension and lem-weil-pbc-tensor-quotient-base-change
   identify their scalar extensions with B and B/(f), and likewise at every later stage.
2. The finite type subalgebras containing A₀ form a small nonempty directed system with
   colimit A. Apply lem-weil-pbc-colimit-flat-ring-map to descend flatness of C₀ after
   enlarging A₀. All later quotients remain flat. The coefficient rings and their finitely
   presented algebras are Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian;
   ℤ is Noetherian since all its ideals are principal.
3. The kernel of the first Koszul differential over B₀ is finitely generated by
   lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-submodule-finite. Choose finitely many generating cycles.
   lem-sfb-koszul-h1-flat-quotient-base-change-surjective applies since C₀ is A₀-flat;
   its pure-tensor formula shows that these classes generate H₁ at every later stage.
4. Since H₁ over B vanishes, each generating cycle becomes a boundary. Choose preimages
   under the second differential. Adjoin the finitely many coefficients of these preimages
   and of polynomial witnesses that their differentials equal the cycles modulo the
   presentation ideal. At this finite coefficient stage every generator is a boundary,
   so the surjection in step 3 makes H₁ vanish there as well.
5. Contract Q to Q₁ in this Noetherian model B₁. Its local ring T is Noetherian by
   lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian. The Koszul base-change isomorphism
   lem-sc-koszul-base-change-iso and lem-weil-pbc-localization-module-exact preserve H₁
   vanishing. Apply lem-per-h1-koszul-quasi-regular and then
   lem-pur-quasi-regular-noetherian-local: the localized elements lie in the maximal ideal,
   and form a regular sequence in T.
6. By lem-pc-affine-flat-module-localization-preserves-flatness and
   lem-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime-of-quotient, T/(f) is A₁-flat. Descend through
   the prefix quotients of this regular sequence. Each is a nonzero Noetherian local ring,
   and the next element is a nonzerodivisor in its maximal ideal. Apply
   lem-sfb-noetherian-local-nonzerodivisor-flat-lift at each step to conclude T is A₁-flat.
   For the empty sequence the initial flatness is already the desired one.
7. Apply lem-weil-pbc-openness-of-flatness to A₁ → B₁ and the rank-one free module B₁.
   The prime Q₁ belongs to this open flat locus. Use
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open to choose h₁ outside Q₁ whose basic open
   lies in that locus. The stalks of (B₁)[1/h₁] are flat; use
   lem-weil-pbc-flat-over-localization and lem-weil-pbc-flat-map-local-criterion to deduce
   A₁-flatness of (B₁)[1/h₁].
8. Map h₁ to h in B; contraction of Q ensures h is outside Q. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-flat-module-base-change and
   lem-weil-pbc-algebra-localization-away-base-change to identify its flat scalar extension
   with B[1/h]. If B is zero or (f) is the unit ideal there is no such Q. If r = 0 the
   quotient hypothesis already gives flatness of B and one may take h = 1. -/
theorem exists_flat_away_of_isH1Regular_flat_quotient
    {A : Type u} {B : Type v} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    [Algebra.FinitePresentation A B] (r : ℕ) (f : Fin r → B)
    (hH1 : RingTheory.Sequence.IsH1Regular f)
    (hflat : Module.Flat A (B ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f)))
    (Q : Ideal B) [Q.IsPrime] (hIQ : Ideal.span (Set.range f) ≤ Q) :
    ∃ h : B, h ∉ Q ∧ Module.Flat A (Localization.Away h) := by
  sorry

end RingTheory
